Subject: [PATCH] webgl: shader precision from the profile

getShaderPrecisionFormat() returns the ranges and precision in the
profile's gl_precisions table, for WebGL1 and WebGL2 on both GPU
decoders. Without it, a page sees SwiftShader's reduced lowp and mediump
values next to limits and extensions that claim a desktop GPU. Desktop
Chrome on real hardware reports Chromium's own 127/127/23 float values,
and SwiftShader computes every qualifier at full float precision, so the
claim is served without a clamp. An entry must carry all three numbers
or it is ignored.

diff --git a/base/apostate/profile.h b/base/apostate/profile.h
index a077b58..35ee265 100644
--- a/base/apostate/profile.h
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.h
@@ -214,6 +214,35 @@ class BASE_EXPORT Profile {
   // be on the real device.
   const std::set<std::string>& gl_extensions() const { return gl_extensions_; }
 
+  // One getShaderPrecisionFormat() answer: the exponent range and mantissa
+  // bits a shader type reports for a precision qualifier.
+  struct ShaderPrecision {
+    int range_min = 0;
+    int range_max = 0;
+    int precision = 0;
+  };
+
+  // getShaderPrecisionFormat() results, keyed "<SHADER>.<QUALIFIER>" exactly as
+  // the collector spells them — "FRAGMENT_SHADER.LOW_FLOAT" and so on.
+  //
+  // This is the third component of the WebGL identity, independent of the
+  // limits above and of the extension list, and it is the one a profile is
+  // most likely to leave on the host: nothing else reads it, so it stays wrong
+  // quietly while the other two are fixed. Matching two of three is worse than
+  // matching none, because a mismatched third component is a contradiction
+  // rather than an unknown.
+  //
+  // The values are not a device property so much as a backend one. Chromium
+  // seeds the desktop answer itself in QueryShaderPrecisionFormat — 127/127/23
+  // for every float qualifier, 31/30/0 for every int — and then calls the
+  // driver, which overwrites it only where the driver implements the entry
+  // point. Desktop GL leaves it a stub, so real Chrome on real hardware
+  // reports Chromium's constants; ANGLE implements it and reports what its
+  // backend declares. That is the entire divergence.
+  const std::map<std::string, ShaderPrecision>& gl_precisions() const {
+    return gl_precisions_;
+  }
+
   // IANA timezone name, e.g. "Europe/Warsaw".
   //
   // Only the zone *name* is a host fact. ICU's data is compiled into the binary
@@ -299,6 +328,7 @@ class BASE_EXPORT Profile {
   std::map<std::string, std::string> keyboard_layout_map_;
   std::map<std::string, int> gl_limits_;
   std::set<std::string> gl_extensions_;
+  std::map<std::string, ShaderPrecision> gl_precisions_;
   std::optional<std::string> timezone_;
   std::optional<bool> prefers_dark_;
   std::optional<std::string> accept_languages_;
